            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: ಎರಡನೆಯ",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಎರಡನೆಯ (eraḍaneya) — \"second\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "ಒಂದು, ಎರಡು, ಮೂರು answer how many. This word answers which one in the line, and Kannada keeps a second set of words for that job."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It goes straight in front of what it counts, and nothing on the noun changes: ಎರಡನೆಯ ದಿನ (eraḍaneya dina), the second day; ಎರಡನೆಯ ಬಾಗಿಲು (eraḍaneya bāgilu), the second door."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the ending, and why the chapter opens on two",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at the word against the number it comes from:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "cardinal",
                "ordinal"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 1,
              "utterances": [
                "cardinal: ಎರಡು (eraḍu). ordinal: ಎರಡನೆಯ (eraḍaneya)."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The final -ು goes, and -ಅನೆಯ (-aneya) goes on. That is the rule, and in Kannada it has no exceptions at all — every number you know already takes it."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the chapter does not open on one. It opens on two, because eraḍu is the first number that shows the ending working, and because the one word that will surprise you is first — which is not built on ಒಂದು at all. An exception is only worth looking at once you can see what it is an exception to."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "eraḍu, then eraḍaneya",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *eraḍu*, then *eraḍaneya*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "eraḍaneya dina — the second day",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *eraḍaneya dina* — the second day]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "eraḍu is how many, eraḍaneya is which one",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *eraḍu* is how many, *eraḍaneya* is which one]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does ಎರಡನೆಯ (eraḍaneya) mean? (Second — which one in the line.) What did you do to ಎರಡು to get it? (Dropped the final -ು, added -ಅನೆಯ.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
